  "preview": "A store stocks two brands of trash bags. Different counts, different prices, different bag sizes. Which is the better buy — and what does 'better' even mean?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Use unit-rate reasoning to compare two products that aren't directly comparable, and notice that 'better buy' depends on what you're optimizing for.",
    "studentPrompt": "A store sells two brands of trash bags.\n\n- Brand A: 80 bags for $14.40\n- Brand B: 36 bags for $7.56\n\n1. Which brand costs less per bag?\n2. Brand A bags hold 13 gallons. Brand B bags hold 30 gallons. Now which is the better buy?\n3. What if a customer needs to take out the trash 50 times this month? Which brand should they buy, and how much will it cost?\n4. Make up your own twist on 'better buy' that changes which brand wins. Defend your answer.",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/27175870/pexels-photo-27175870.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "A man wearing a mask shops in a supermarket, selecting items from a refrigerated section.",
      "credit": "Photo by Helena Lopes on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Hold up two real boxes if you have them, or just describe them. Tell the story. Then ask 'which is the better buy?' Take guesses without doing math. Then turn them loose.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: actual trash bag boxes"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "unit-rate",
      "ratios",
      "comparison",
      "consumer-math"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. Low floor (anyone can divide), high ceiling (multi-criterion comparison in Q4). Real context, debate-worthy.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Divide price by count for each: $14.40 ÷ 80 = $0.18/bag, $7.56 ÷ 36 = $0.21/bag.\n- Some groups will compute the inverse (bags per dollar) and reach the same conclusion differently.\n- For Q2, students must compute price per gallon: A = $0.18 / 13 ≈ $0.0138/gal, B = $0.21 / 30 = $0.007/gal. The bigger bag wins.\n- For Q3, '50 times' is fewer than 36, so they only need one box. Brand B at $7.56 vs Brand A at $14.40 → Brand B wins on flat cost — but Brand A's 80 bags would last longer per dollar.\n- Q4 invites scenarios: small household needs few bags, two-person household with mostly small loads, etc.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Comparing total prices ('Brand B is cheaper') without normalizing to per-bag.\n- Stopping at Q1 and assuming Q2 has the same answer.\n- Dividing the wrong way (count ÷ price gives bags-per-dollar, also valid but easy to misread).",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Why didn't the answer to Q1 stay the answer for Q2? What changed?\n- Two groups divided differently (price/bag vs bags/dollar). Are both right? What's different?\n- In Q3, you only buy ONE box. Did the unit rate matter then?\n- 'Better buy' depends on context. When does the lower price-per-unit lose to a different criterion?",
    "extensions": "- A third store sells Brand C: 200 bags for $32, but the bags only hold 8 gallons. Does Brand C beat both? On what criterion?\n- The store offers a 'buy 3, get 1 free' on Brand A. What's the new effective unit rate? What about a 20% off coupon on Brand B?\n- Suppose you generate one 13-gallon bag of trash per day and one 30-gallon bag per week. Plan a purchase for 6 months.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. A = $14.40/80 = $0.18 per bag. B = $7.56/36 = $0.21 per bag. Brand A is cheaper per bag.\n2. A: $0.18/13 gal ≈ $0.0138/gal. B: $0.21/30 gal = $0.007/gal. Brand B is cheaper per gallon of capacity (about half).\n3. 50 bags exceeds Brand B's count (36), so they need 2 boxes of B (= $15.12, total 72 bags) OR 1 box of A ($14.40, 80 bags). Brand A wins.\n4. Open. Examples: 'I only have a small kitchen trash can — Brand A is better because I can't fill 30-gallon bags.' / 'I'm a contractor and need huge bags — Brand B at half the price per gallon is the clear winner.'",
